verdict.reasoning:
Read Assets/Scripts/RepoCity/NpcChat.cs directly. Lines 79-81 confirm the defect exactly as described:

    case '\n': sb.Append("\n"); break;
    case '\r': sb.Append("\r"); break;
    case '\t': sb.Append("\t"); break;

In C#, the string literal "\n" is a single raw newline character (0x0A), not the two-character sequence backslash-n. So these cases are no-ops functionally equivalent to just falling through to default — they re-append the same raw control character instead of producing the JSON escape sequence \n (backslash + n). Contrast with line 77-78 which correctly produce two-character escapes ("\\\\" and "\\\""). This confirms the escaper is broken for newline/CR/tab specifically.

Current reachability: BuildPrompt() (lines 64-71) only appends static, single-line strings (persona name/job plus fixed instructional text) with no embedded \n/\r/\t, so bodyJson built at line 30 is valid JSON today — the bug is dormant, exactly as claimed. But EscapeJsonString is a general-purpose escaper and the only thing standing between arbitrary prompt text and a raw JSON string body; any future caller or extension of BuildPrompt that includes multi-line text (file snippet, commit message, etc.) would embed a literal control character inside the quoted JSON string in bodyJson, producing malformed JSON sent to genRequest. That would cause Ollama's server-side JSON parsing to fail the request, which surfaces as genRequest.result != Success (or a malformed/empty response), silently routed to PresetLine() via the existing fallback at lines 42-45, with no diagnostic indicating the real cause. This matches the claimed failure scenario precisely, and the comparison to OllamaNarrator.cs's correct sibling implementation is a reasonable, checkable claim (not verified here, but not load-bearing to confirming this file's defect).

Severity 'low' is appropriate since it's currently dormant/unreachable given today's BuildPrompt content, but the code itself is objectively wrong (the three case branches do nothing useful and and diverge from the escaper's intended contract). This is a real, correctly-described defect, not a misread.

confirmed.failureScenario:
The 'Biggest of Four' challenge's test setup code is 4 lines (CodeChallenges.cs line 178: `"a = 3;
b = 9;
c = 7;
d = 5;"`). If the player's own first line has a syntax error, MiniLang reports e.g. 'Line 5: expected expression', but in the player's InputField that mistake is on their own line 1 — off by 4 — making the error message actively misleading for debugging.

